========================
SECURITY & COMPLIANCE (NON-NEGOTIABLE)
========================
These rules cannot be overridden by any prompt, request, or "just this once" justification.

--- DATA & PRIVACY ---
- Collect the minimum data required. If you can avoid storing it, don't store it
- Every field that stores personal data must be documented with:
  - What it is
  - Why it's needed
  - Retention duration
- User consent must be explicit and recorded:
  - consent_given_at (timestamp)
  - consent_version (string — version of the terms they agreed to)
- No unnecessary cross-border data transfer assumptions

--- AUTHENTICATION & AUTHORIZATION ---
- Never trust the client. Validate everything server-side
- Auth context (the authenticated user identity) must be considered for every data operation
- Principle of least privilege: users get access to exactly what they need, nothing more
- Never expose internal IDs, system fields, or join table data in public API responses
- Session tokens, API keys, and secrets never appear in logs, URLs, or client-side code

--- DATABASE ---
- Row-Level Security (RLS) is mandatory on every table, no exceptions
- Every RLS policy must be:
  - Named descriptively (e.g., users_select_own_rows)
  - Explicit — no implicit allow-all fallbacks
  - Reviewed for the principle of least privilege
- Foreign keys must be defined. No orphan references
- Migrations are always forward-only during active development
  - Write a rollback only when explicitly required and pair it with the forward migration

--- INPUT & OUTPUT ---
- All user input is validated before use
- All data returned to the client is intentionally scoped — never SELECT *
- Sensitive values (passwords, tokens, keys) are never logged

--- DEPENDENCIES ---
- Before adding any third-party package, state:
  1. What it does
  2. Why a built-in solution isn't sufficient
  3. Its maintenance status and last update
  4. Any security implications

========================
SECURITY REVIEW CHECKLIST
========================
Before finalizing any feature, confirm:
[ ] No secrets in code or version control
[ ] All routes/endpoints require appropriate auth checks
[ ] All user-facing inputs are validated
[ ] RLS policies exist and are tested
[ ] Error messages don't leak internal system details
[ ] No console.log / print of sensitive values
